# Tool Review and Cycle Limits

## Overview

Your support Agent can issue refunds, and nobody wants a model to move money
without a person signing off. You'll make ReAct stop before
`MyApp.IssueRefund` runs, approve one refund and deny another, and then
bound the loop with tool-round limits and repeated-call detection. By the
end, this works offline:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
:ok = Jido.AgentServer.deliver_wait(server, wait.id, approval)
```

and the refund runs only after that line.

| Part | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| `tool_policy.review` | Names the tools that need approval in this request. |
| `Jido.AI.Reasoning.ReAct.ToolReview` | The Flow that waits for `approved`, `denied`, or a 5-minute timer. |
| `jido.ai.review.requested` | The Signal your approval UI receives. |
| `limits.tool_rounds` | Stops the loop after N tool rounds. |
| Cycle detection | Warns the model when it repeats the same calls. |

Review needs a live Agent. A waiting turn suspends inside an AgentServer and
resumes when you deliver a decision.

## Install

```elixir
Mix.install([{:jido_ai, "~> 3.0.0-beta.1"}, {:plug, "~> 1.0"}])
```

## Define the Agent

```elixir
defmodule MyApp.LookupOrder do
  use Jido.Action,
    id: "my_app/lookup_order",
    name: "lookup_order",
    description: "Look up the shipping status of one order.",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{order_id: Zoi.string()}),
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  @impl Jido.Action
  def run(%{order_id: order_id}, _context) do
    {:ok, %{order_id: order_id, status: "shipped", eta: "2026-11-02"}}
  end
end

defmodule MyApp.IssueRefund do
  use Jido.Action,
    id: "my_app/issue_refund",
    name: "issue_refund",
    description: "Refund part or all of one order.",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{order_id: Zoi.string(), amount_cents: Zoi.integer(gte: 1)}),
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  @impl Jido.Action
  def run(%{order_id: order_id, amount_cents: amount}, _context) do
    {:ok, %{refund_id: "R-#{order_id}", amount_cents: amount}}
  end
end

defmodule MyApp.SupportAgent do
  use Jido.AI.Agent,
    name: "support_agent",
    id: "my_app/support_agent",
    vsn: 1

  ai do
    reasoning Jido.AI.Reasoning.ReAct do
      model "openai:gpt-4o-mini"
    end

    tools do
      action MyApp.LookupOrder
      action MyApp.IssueRefund
    end
  end
end
```

## Offline Model

The model runs inside the AgentServer process, so the stubs use shared mode:

```elixir
defmodule MyApp.FakeOpenAI do
  def function_call(call_id, name, arguments) do
    response([
      %{
        "type" => "function_call",
        "id" => "fc-#{call_id}",
        "call_id" => call_id,
        "name" => name,
        "arguments" => Jason.encode!(arguments),
        "status" => "completed"
      }
    ])
  end

  def text(text) do
    response([
      %{
        "type" => "message",
        "id" => "msg-text",
        "role" => "assistant",
        "status" => "completed",
        "content" => [%{"type" => "output_text", "text" => text, "annotations" => []}]
      }
    ])
  end

  def stub(body), do: Req.Test.expect(MyApp.LLM, &Req.Test.json(&1, body))

  defp response(output) do
    %{
      "id" => "resp-#{System.unique_integer([:positive])}",
      "object" => "response",
      "created_at" => 1,
      "model" => "gpt-4o-mini-2024-07-18",
      "status" => "completed",
      "error" => nil,
      "incomplete_details" => nil,
      "parallel_tool_calls" => true,
      "output" => output,
      "usage" => %{
        "input_tokens" => 20,
        "input_tokens_details" => %{"cached_tokens" => 0},
        "output_tokens" => 5,
        "output_tokens_details" => %{"reasoning_tokens" => 0},
        "total_tokens" => 25
      }
    }
  end
end

Req.Test.set_req_test_to_shared()

offline = %{
  api_key: "offline-test-key",
  max_retries: 0,
  req_http_options: [plug: {Req.Test, MyApp.LLM}]
}
```

## Review Needs a Wait Host

`cmd/2` runs the Flow as plain data. It can't suspend, so a review request
stops before the model runs:

```elixir
agent = MyApp.SupportAgent.instantiate!(id: "support-data")

request =
  MyApp.SupportAgent.request_signal!(%{
    input: "Refund order A-1001",
    options: offline,
    tool_policy: %{review: ["issue_refund"]}
  })

{:ok, _agent, [%Jido.Agent.Directive.Reply{signal: reply}]} =
  MyApp.SupportAgent.cmd(agent, request)

Map.take(reply.data, [:status, :reason_code, :reason, :model_calls])
```

The result is
`%{status: :blocked, reason_code: :review, reason: "execution host does not support waits", model_calls: 0}`.

## Start a Live Agent

```elixir
{:ok, _jido} = Jido.start(namespace: "my_app/support")

{:ok, server} =
  Jido.start_agent(MyApp.SupportAgent,
    id: "support-live",
    persistence: {Jido.Persistence.ETS, table: :support_reviews},
    turn_timeout: :infinity
  )

:ok = Jido.AgentServer.await_ready(server)
```

| Option | Why |
| --- | --- |
| `namespace:` | Persistence keys need a stable instance namespace. Without one, the Agent stops with `:stable_namespace_required`. |
| `persistence:` | A wait is part of a durable turn. Without persistence, the turn can't suspend and review is blocked the same way as with `cmd/2`. `Jido.Persistence.ETS` keeps it in memory for this notebook. |
| `turn_timeout: :infinity` | The default turn timeout is 60,000 ms, wait time included. Let the review timer own expiry instead. |

## Request a Refund

Queue the model's refund call:

```elixir
MyApp.FakeOpenAI.stub(
  MyApp.FakeOpenAI.function_call("call-refund", "issue_refund", %{
    order_id: "A-1001",
    amount_cents: 2_500
  })
)
```

Submit the request. `tool_policy.review` marks `issue_refund` for approval.
`jido_ai_dispatch` in the execution context tells ReAct where to send the
review request. A durable turn stores its context, so the target must be
portable: a registered name, not a pid. This notebook registers itself as the
review inbox:

```elixir
Process.register(self(), :review_inbox)

request =
  MyApp.SupportAgent.request_signal!(%{
    input: "Refund $25 on order A-1001",
    options: offline,
    tool_policy: %{review: ["issue_refund"], reason: "refunds need a supervisor"}
  })

pending =
  Jido.AgentServer.submit(server, request,
    context: %{jido_ai_dispatch: {:named, target: {:name, :review_inbox}}}
  )
```

`submit/3` returns at once. The turn calls the model, sees `issue_refund`,
and suspends.

## Read the Review Request

```elixir
review =
  receive do
    {:signal, %Jido.Signal{type: "jido.ai.review.requested"} = signal} -> signal.data
  after
    10_000 -> :no_review
  end

Map.take(review, [:review_id, :options, :policy_reason, :tool_calls])
```

The result is:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
%{
  review_id: "review:run_...:1",
  options: [:approved, :denied],
  policy_reason: "refunds need a supervisor",
  tool_calls: [
    %{
      id: "call-refund",
      name: "issue_refund",
      arguments: %{"amount_cents" => 2500, "order_id" => "A-1001"}
    }
  ]
}
```

The Signal also carries `request_id`, `run_id`, `session_id`, `thread_id`,
and `expires_at_ms`, which is 300,000 ms after the request. Show the calls
to the reviewer. Nothing has run yet.

ReAct sends the Signal just before the turn suspends. Poll `status/2` until
the wait is open:

```elixir
open_wait = fn server ->
  Enum.find_value(1..3_000, fn _ ->
    case Jido.AgentServer.status(server) do
      %{waits: [wait]} -> wait
      _status -> Process.sleep(10) && nil
    end
  end)
end

wait = open_wait.(server)

{is_binary(wait.id), Enum.map(wait.options, & &1.name)}
```

The result is `{true, ["approved", "denied", "expired"]}`.

## Approve

Queue the model's final answer, then deliver an approval. The Signal `data`
must contain the same `review_id`, `request_id`, and `run_id`. `actor_id`
and `reason` are optional and go into the record:

```elixir
MyApp.FakeOpenAI.stub(MyApp.FakeOpenAI.text("Refund R-A-1001 for $25.00 is issued."))

approval =
  Jido.Signal.new!(
    "jido.ai.review.approved",
    review
    |> Map.take([:review_id, :request_id, :run_id])
    |> Map.merge(%{actor_id: "supervisor-7", reason: "within policy"}),
    source: "/my_app/review_ui"
  )

:ok = Jido.AgentServer.deliver_wait(server, wait.id, approval)

{:ok, _agent, [%Jido.Agent.Directive.Reply{signal: reply}]} =
  Jido.AgentServer.await(pending, 30_000)

{reply.data.status, reply.data.output, hd(reply.data.tool_attempts).review}
```

The result is
`{:completed, "Refund R-A-1001 for $25.00 is issued.", %{required: true, status: :approved}}`.

The Session records both sides of the review as `:ai_review` entries:

```elixir
session = Jido.AgentServer.agent(server).state.session

session.thread
|> Jido.Thread.filter_by_kind(:ai_review)
|> Enum.map(&{&1.payload["status"], &1.payload["decision"]})
```

The result is
`[{"requested", nil}, {"approved", %{"actor_id" => "supervisor-7", "reason" => "within policy", ...}}]`.

## Deny

A denial ends the request. The tool doesn't run and the model isn't called
again:

```elixir
MyApp.FakeOpenAI.stub(
  MyApp.FakeOpenAI.function_call("call-refund-2", "issue_refund", %{
    order_id: "A-1002",
    amount_cents: 90_000
  })
)

pending =
  Jido.AgentServer.submit(server, request,
    context: %{jido_ai_dispatch: {:named, target: {:name, :review_inbox}}}
  )

review =
  receive do
    {:signal, %Jido.Signal{type: "jido.ai.review.requested"} = signal} -> signal.data
  after
    10_000 -> :no_review
  end

wait = open_wait.(server)

denial =
  Jido.Signal.new!(
    "jido.ai.review.denied",
    Map.take(review, [:review_id, :request_id, :run_id]),
    source: "/my_app/review_ui"
  )

:ok = Jido.AgentServer.deliver_wait(server, wait.id, denial)

{:ok, _agent, [%Jido.Agent.Directive.Reply{signal: reply}]} =
  Jido.AgentServer.await(pending, 30_000)

Map.take(reply.data, [:status, :reason_code, :reason, :tool_calls, :model_calls])
```

The result is
`%{status: :blocked, reason_code: :review, reason: "tool use was denied", tool_calls: 0, model_calls: 1}`.

## Timeouts

The `expired` option is a 300,000 ms timer that starts when the wait opens.
When it fires first, the request ends with
`status: :blocked, reason_code: :review, reason: "tool review expired"`.
The timeout is fixed in `Jido.AI.Reasoning.ReAct.ToolReview`. The request
schema has no field for it.

A review wait is durable. With AgentServer persistence configured, the wait
survives a hibernate or restart and keeps the same wait ID. Deliver the
decision to the thawed server, and the same graph resumes without repeating
the model call.

## Review Scope

| Request field | Tools reviewed |
| --- | --- |
| `tool_policy: %{review: ["issue_refund"]}` | Only `issue_refund` |
| `review_tools: true` | Every visible tool |

Review is per round. When any call in a model response needs review, the
whole round waits, and approval runs every call in it. Each later round that
calls a reviewed tool asks again, with a new `review_id` ending in the round
number. A name in `review` must be visible in the request, or the request
fails with `{:review_tool_not_visible, name}`.

Stop the live Agent before the loop examples:

```elixir
:ok = Jido.stop_agent("support-live")
```

## Limit Tool Rounds

`limits.tool_rounds` (default `4`, range `0..8`) caps how many rounds one
request can run. When the model asks for a round past the limit, the request
ends before those calls run:

```elixir
MyApp.FakeOpenAI.stub(
  MyApp.FakeOpenAI.function_call("call-1", "lookup_order", %{order_id: "A-1001"})
)

MyApp.FakeOpenAI.stub(
  MyApp.FakeOpenAI.function_call("call-2", "lookup_order", %{order_id: "A-1002"})
)

agent = MyApp.SupportAgent.instantiate!(id: "support-rounds")

request =
  MyApp.SupportAgent.request_signal!(%{
    input: "Check orders A-1001 and A-1002",
    options: offline,
    limits: %{tool_rounds: 1}
  })

{:ok, _agent, [%Jido.Agent.Directive.Reply{signal: reply}]} =
  MyApp.SupportAgent.cmd(agent, request)

Map.take(reply.data, [:status, :reason_code, :reason, :tool_rounds, :tool_calls, :model_calls])
```

The result is
`%{status: :blocked, reason_code: :limits, reason: "maximum tool rounds reached", tool_rounds: 1, tool_calls: 1, model_calls: 2}`.

`limits.tool_calls` (default `8`) caps the total calls in the request the
same way, with `"maximum tool calls reached"`.

## Repeated Calls

`Jido.AI.Reasoning.ReAct.Actions.DetectToolCycle` runs after every model
response. When a round has the same tool names and arguments as the round
before, ignoring call IDs, it counts one cycle and adds a system message to
the next model call:

```text
The previous tool calls repeated with the same arguments. Do not repeat them again. Use the available results for a final answer, or use a different approach.
```

The model asks for `A-1001` twice in a row, then answers:

```elixir
MyApp.FakeOpenAI.stub(
  MyApp.FakeOpenAI.function_call("call-1", "lookup_order", %{order_id: "A-1001"})
)

MyApp.FakeOpenAI.stub(
  MyApp.FakeOpenAI.function_call("call-2", "lookup_order", %{order_id: "A-1001"})
)

MyApp.FakeOpenAI.stub(MyApp.FakeOpenAI.text("Order A-1001 has shipped."))

agent = MyApp.SupportAgent.instantiate!(id: "support-cycle")

request =
  MyApp.SupportAgent.request_signal!(%{
    input: "Where is order A-1001?",
    options: offline
  })

{:ok, agent, [%Jido.Agent.Directive.Reply{signal: reply}]} =
  MyApp.SupportAgent.cmd(agent, request)

Map.take(reply.data, [:status, :tool_rounds, :tool_calls, :tool_cycles, :model_calls])
```

The result is
`%{status: :completed, tool_rounds: 2, tool_calls: 2, tool_cycles: 1, model_calls: 3}`.

Cycle detection warns. It doesn't stop the request or skip the repeated
calls: the second round ran. A model that ignores the warning keeps going
until `limits.tool_rounds`, `limits.tool_calls`, or `limits.model_calls`
stops it. Read `tool_cycles` in the reply, or in the `:ai_request_outcome`
entry, to find requests that looped.

## Clean Up

```elixir
:ok = Jido.stop()
```

## Summary

- `tool_policy.review` or `review_tools: true` makes ReAct wait before a
  round with a reviewed tool runs.
- Review needs an AgentServer. `cmd/2` returns
  `"execution host does not support waits"`.
- The `jido.ai.review.requested` Signal goes to the `jido_ai_dispatch` target
  in the execution context.
- Deliver `jido.ai.review.approved` or `jido.ai.review.denied` with matching
  `review_id`, `request_id`, and `run_id` through
  `Jido.AgentServer.deliver_wait/3`.
- An unanswered review expires after 300,000 ms.
- `limits.tool_rounds` and `limits.tool_calls` stop new tool work with
  `reason_code: :limits`.
- Repeated identical rounds add a warning and count in `tool_cycles`. They
  don't stop the request.

